\title{\textbf{Discrete Drainage Dynamics:\\
A Bounded, Conservative Local Rule for\\
Newtonian-Like Potentials}}
\author[1]{Stanislas Dewavrin}
\affil[1]{Independent researcher \\ \texttt{dewavrin.iphone@gmail.com}}
\date{April 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\noindent
We define a deterministic, parallel, local update rule on a
discrete graph. Each node carries a non-negative reserve
$R_i \ge 0$, with rest value $R_0$ at equilibrium. Each
oriented link carries a non-negative directional flux. The rule
alternates a node update and a link update, with the outgoing
fluxes from a node rate-limited so that the reserve cannot
become negative. The flux on a link is written in a general
form involving a \emph{mobility function} $\mu(R)$ which encodes
how the source node's reserve modulates its emission rate. The
present paper adopts the constant-mobility baseline
$\mu \equiv 1$, the simplest choice consistent with the
substrate's locality and conservation properties. In this
baseline, far from the non-negativity floor, the rule's steady
state coincides with the discrete Poisson equation, and we
measure on the lattice that the static profile around a point
source follows a $1/r$ law with the continuum coefficient at
the percent level. Companion papers study other mobility
prescriptions and their non-linear consequences.
\end{abstract}

\section{What this paper claims}
\label{sec:claims}

We make three claims.

\begin{enumerate}
\item We define a deterministic, parallel, two-step local rule
on a discrete graph. The reserve is bounded below by zero,
$R_0$ is the rest value, and the flux on a link involves a
mobility function $\mu(R)$ that we leave as a structural choice
of the framework. The present paper studies the
constant-mobility baseline $\mu \equiv 1$.
\item Run as a stationary linearised simulation with a point
source on a cubic lattice, the rule (with $\mu \equiv 1$)
produces a radial profile compatible with $1/r$ in the bulk,
matching the discrete continuum prediction at the percent level
across $L \in \{64, 128, 160\}$.
\item The internal flux-exchange rule preserves total reserve
exactly. Non-negativity of the reserve is enforced by
rate-limiting the outgoing fluxes and external sinks of a node
in proportion. This is the structural feature that
distinguishes the rule from a plain discrete heat equation.
\end{enumerate}

We do not claim the following. We do not derive Newton's
gravitational constant, general relativity, or any property of
the Standard Model. We do not derive the mobility function
$\mu(R)$; the constant-mobility baseline of this paper is a
choice within a family of possibilities. We do not assert that
the $1/r$ result is itself novel: it is not. What is novel is
the rule, the mobility framing, and the structural features
that make the rule non-trivial in the regimes explored in the
companion papers.

\section{The picture, in plain words}
\label{sec:picture}

Imagine a fine three-dimensional grid. At each grid point sits
a reservoir holding a non-negative amount of stuff, with rest
level $R_0$. Reservoirs can be excited above $R_0$ or drained
below it, but they cannot go below zero. Between neighbours
run pipes that carry flow from the fuller reservoir to the
emptier one, at a rate proportional to the level difference
and optionally modulated by the source level. A reservoir
cannot deliver more than it holds.

If we start at rest level everywhere, drain one point, and let
it settle, with $\mu \equiv 1$ the bulk pattern is the
textbook discrete Poisson solution, $\delta(r) = R_0 - R(r)$
falling as $1/r$. Other mobility choices give different
non-linear regimes (companion papers).

\section{The substrate}
\label{sec:substrate}

The substrate is a graph with at least six neighbours per node
and statistical isotropy. We use cubic lattice in simulations.
Each node $i$ carries $R_i \ge 0$. Each oriented link carries
non-negative directional fluxes $F_{i \to j}$.

The full rule of the framework also assigns angular quadratures
$(T_i, I_i)$ with $T_i^2 + I_i^2 \le R_i$ and link phases
$\psi_{ij}$, used in the gauge sector (Papers~V--VI). They do
not enter the present paper.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Parameter & Role & Used in this paper\\
\midrule
$\kappa$    & drainage coefficient            & yes\\
$\alpha$    & flux propagation coefficient    & yes\\
$R_0$       & reserve rest value              & yes\\
$\mu(R)$    & mobility function               & yes ($\mu \equiv 1$)\\
$J$         & link relaxation coupling        & no (deferred)\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Parameters of the rule. The mobility $\mu(R)$ is a
structural choice: this paper uses $\mu \equiv 1$, Paper~II
uses $\mu(R) = R/R_0$.}
\label{tab:params}
\end{table}

\section{The two-step tick}
\label{sec:tick}

The rule decomposes into a Link Update followed by a Node
Update, alternating each tick. Locality is preserved (each
update reads only nearest neighbours). Two-step alternation is
a standard local lattice construction.

\subsection{Link Update}

For each oriented link $(i, j)$ in parallel, compute a desired
flux,
\begin{equation}
F_{i \to j}^{\rm des} \;=\; \alpha\, \mu(R_i)\, (R_i - R_j)_+,
\label{eq:fluxdes}
\end{equation}
where $\mu(R)$ is a non-negative mobility function with
$\mu(R_0) = 1$. We do not derive $\mu(R)$ from a deeper
principle; it is a structural choice of the rule.

\paragraph{Constant-mobility baseline of this paper.} We adopt
\begin{equation}
\mu(R) \;\equiv\; 1,
\label{eq:mu_const}
\end{equation}
for which Eq.~\eqref{eq:fluxdes} reduces to
$F_{i \to j}^{\rm des} = \alpha (R_i - R_j)_+$, and the
linearised steady state is the discrete Poisson equation.

\paragraph{Other mobility choices in companion papers.} The
reserve-proportional mobility $\mu(R) = R/R_0$, motivated by
local bandwidth limitation at the source, is studied in
Paper~II and produces $\nabla^2(R^2) = 0$ in the continuum
limit, yielding a Schwarzschild-form profile. Other choices
may be explored elsewhere.

Each node computes a rate-limiter
\begin{equation}
A_i = R_i + \tau S_i^{\rm in}, \quad
S_i^{\rm in} = \sum_j F_{j \to i}^{\rm des}, \quad
D_i = \tau O_i^{\rm des} + \tau \kappa E_i, \quad
O_i^{\rm des} = \sum_j F_{i \to j}^{\rm des},
\label{eq:budgets}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\beta_i = \begin{cases} 1, & D_i = 0,\\
\min(1, A_i/D_i), & D_i > 0, \end{cases}
\label{eq:beta}
\end{equation}
applied identically to outflows and sink:
$F_{i \to j} = \beta_i F_{i \to j}^{\rm des}$,
$E_i^{\rm real} = \beta_i E_i$.

\subsection{Node Update}

\begin{equation}
R_i \leftarrow R_i + \tau S_i^{\rm in} - \beta_i \tau O_i^{\rm des} - \beta_i \tau \kappa E_i.
\label{eq:nodeR}
\end{equation}
By construction, $R_i \ge 0$ at every tick.

\section{Conservation}
\label{sec:conservation}

Internal flux exchange conserves total reserve exactly: realised
fluxes enter Node Update of $j$ as $+\beta_i F_{i \to j}^{\rm des}$
and Node Update of $i$ as $-\beta_i F_{i \to j}^{\rm des}$.
External sinks $E_i^{\rm real} = \beta_i E_i$ are explicit
bookkeeping. The closed system network-plus-sink-reservoir is
conservative.

\section{Simulation: from rule to $1/r$}
\label{sec:sim}

\subsection{Derivation of the Poisson limit (constant mobility)}

With $\mu \equiv 1$ and $\beta_i = 1$, the two directional fluxes
satisfy $F_{i \to j} - F_{j \to i} = \alpha(R_i - R_j)$, so
$S_i = \alpha \Delta R_i$. Stationary regime gives
$\alpha \Delta R_i = \kappa E_i$, and with $\delta_i = R_0 - R_i$:
\begin{equation}
-\Delta \delta_i = M \delta_{\rm Kr}(i, i_0), \quad M = \kappa E_0 / \alpha.
\label{eq:poisson}
\end{equation}
Continuum solution $\delta(r) = M/(4\pi r)$.

\subsection{Numerical check}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lllll}
\toprule
$L$ & $A_{\rm fit}$ & rel.\ bias & precision & $N_{\rm iter}$\\
\midrule
64  & 0.07878 & $-1.01\%$ & $7.0 \times 10^{-5}$ & 5\,000\\
128 & 0.07866 & $-1.16\%$ & $5.7 \times 10^{-6}$ & 10\,000\\
160 & 0.07886 & $-0.90\%$ & $3.7 \times 10^{-6}$ & 30\,000\\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{l}{$A_{\rm continuum} = M/(4\pi)$} & $0.07958$ & & \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Newton-fit on cubic lattices, $M = 1$, Dirichlet
boundary, $\mu \equiv 1$.}
\label{tab:gres}
\end{table}

\subsection{Robustness: spectral dimension}

Spectral dimension converges to $3$ as $L$ grows (cubic
$d_s - 3$ minima: $0.129, 0.054, 0.023$ at $L = 32, 64, 128$).
The gravitational sector is Newtonian in the IR limit.

\subsection{Code and data}

\url{https://github.com/stanislasdewavrin/DDD-Paper-1-Foundations}.
Pure-numpy.

\section{Calibration vs.\ prediction}
\label{sec:cal-vs-pred}

\textbf{Calibration.} The mappings $\delta \leftrightarrow \Phi$
and $E \leftrightarrow m$ are external. The lattice unit and
$\kappa$ are fixed by demanding $A_{\rm fit}$ matches Newton's
$G$.

\textbf{Prediction.} Within that family, the rule with
$\mu \equiv 1$ predicts a static $1/r$ potential.

\section{What the model is not, and what it does not do}
\label{sec:not}

We do not derive general relativity, Newton's $G$, the speed of
light $c$, or any property of the Standard Model.

We do not derive the mobility function $\mu(R)$. The present
paper studies the constant-mobility baseline $\mu \equiv 1$,
for which the linearised steady state is the discrete Poisson
equation. Different mobility choices lead to different
non-linear steady-state equations; the constant-mobility
choice is the minimal Newtonian baseline. Companion papers
study specific reserve-dependent mobility prescriptions, in
particular the source-side bandwidth-throttled choice
$\mu(R) = R/R_0$ in Paper~II.

We have not shown the rule is unique.

\section{Open questions}
\label{sec:open}

\paragraph{Why six neighbours?} Minimal cubic coordination,
not a fundamental requirement.

\paragraph{Why this order, Node then Link?} Microscopic
breaking of time-reversal; not derived.

\paragraph{What sets $R_0$?} Only structural scale of the
rule; not derived.

\paragraph{What sets the mobility $\mu(R)$?} The
constant-mobility baseline of this paper is the simplest
choice. Other choices (notably the bandwidth-throttled
$\mu(R) = R/R_0$ of Paper~II) yield qualitatively different
non-linear regimes. A microscopic argument that selects a
specific mobility from the rule would close an important gap.

\paragraph{Is the rule unique?} See Sec.~\ref{sec:not}.

\paragraph{What sits below the substrate?} Beyond the present
paper.

\section{Outlook}
\label{sec:outlook}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Paper~II --- weak-field gravitational behaviour with the
source-side bandwidth-throttled mobility $\mu(R) = R/R_0$,
yielding the Schwarzschild time-dilation factor.
\emph{Linearised plus saturation; partial.}
\item Paper~III --- discrete-time response and inertial effects.
\emph{Speculative.}
\item Paper~IV --- predictions and tests of the gravitational
sector. \emph{Mixed.}
\item Paper~V --- gauge sector and emergent electromagnetism.
\emph{Speculative.}
\item Further notes (Papers~VI and onward) address coupling,
cosmology, quantum phenomenology, particle structure.
\emph{Increasingly speculative.}
\end{enumerate}

The present paper, Paper~I, claims only the rule itself (with
constant-mobility baseline) and the linearised $1/r$
verification. The rest is what one might do with such a rule.

